00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.040
EUGENE YEH: Hello,
everyone, thanks for coming.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.050
My name's Eugene Yeh.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.110
I lead the GROWTH team
with an Android Enterprise.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.750
That's basically everyone
who's out in the field

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.300
helping partners and
customers, like yourselves, be

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.020
successful with Android
in the enterprise space.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.300
With me today is Nisha
Sharma who's with Accenture.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.070
And we're here to talk
to you a little bit

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:31.050
about how Android is
disrupting the enterprise

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.080
space with mobility.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.380
There's always a good and bad
about being in the latter half

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.300
of the conference.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.300
The bad part, obviously,
is you've absorbed

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.468
a lot of this information.

00:00:44.468 --> 00:00:46.260
And you want to be in
the front because you

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.150
have a lot of energy when you're
coming into the conference,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.050
and you're taking
a lot of notes.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:49.950
And then you get
to the back half,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.400
you're like, oh, overloaded.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.788
But the good news is,
honestly, as you're now

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:57.330
processing all the things you've
heard about for the last two

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.860
or three days and
incorporating that

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:02.010
into what you're supposed to
be doing back at the office,

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.560
you're now realizing,
oh, I've got to go back.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.675
And how am I supposed to use all
these things in the priorities

00:01:07.675 --> 00:01:09.300
that I had set just
like two months ago

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.508
at the beginning of the year?

00:01:10.508 --> 00:01:12.990
And this is really
good context for us

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.700
to talk about
Android Enterprise,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.120
because a lot of folks are
thinking about mobility not

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.250
as a standalone thing but
in the context of what

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.610
you're trying to do more
broadly within your business.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.260
And not surprisingly, when we
go and talk to organizations,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.420
a lot of organizations
are well on their way

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.330
of deploying what they would
term as digital transformation

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.600
technologies.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.030
However, when you
think about that

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.940
more broadly in the
context of the capabilities

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.400
of the organization, you begin
to run into a lot of problems.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.000
And I know when you
hear about more broadly

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.500
within the conference,
a lot of that

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.600
are kind of on-premise
technologies versus cloud

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.780
and things like that.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.870
But even more core to
this is the ability

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.690
to deliver a lot of these
capabilities to mobility.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.490
And when you think
about the fact

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.030
that 41% of the
organizations don't have wall

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.700
to wall mobility, that's
kind of a problem.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.320
When you think
about IoT, when you

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.600
think about the new
things from analytics,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.850
the ability to
have that knowledge

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.310
at the edge of your workforce
and with your customers

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.850
is incredibly important.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.330
The idea that you have
to manage supply chains,

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.270
that you have to
manage intelligence

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.130
without having an
endpoint out there

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.453
of some type from a
mobility perspective

00:02:22.453 --> 00:02:24.870
makes it very difficult for
you to both build applications

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.860
and be successful
in your business.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.450
Because at this point, mobility
or even web technologies,

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.470
they're just a thin
veneer to your business.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.050
They are the interface
for both your customers

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.300
and your employees to
access all the capabilities

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.660
that you've been building
within your organization.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.140
Mobility is actually
how people take action.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.840
It's how people will then
leverage those capabilities

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.610
you've built.
You've built on GCP,

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.818
you've built on your on-premise
premise technologies.

00:02:52.818 --> 00:02:55.080
All of these things
are capabilities

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.920
that you need an actual
device to then get access to.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.230
And so an example
of this is smart

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.380
manufacturing, or more
broadly speaking, just

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:05.400
distribution channels.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.290
This affects retail customers.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.030
This affects
manufacturing customers.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.400
It affects all the
folks in industrial.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.580
An example specifically for us--

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.200
Marks and Spencers'
customer of ours

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.150
that faces this
challenge as well--

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.460
in that you have to
manage your supply chain,

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.840
as I talked about before.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.220
But if you are folks in the
field, your task workers,

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.810
your people who are out actually
in the distribution centers

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.870
don't have a mobile device
as part of your platform,

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.480
you have a challenge, that
they can't scale the barcode,

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.217
if you can't get real time
inventory information,

00:03:36.217 --> 00:03:37.050
you have difficulty.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.600
And people are trying to find
ways around this with RFID

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.280
and other capabilities
with sensors.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.460
But ultimately, you have to
have your workers be enabled.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.190
So the question
then becomes, why

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.570
haven't we done this before?

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.940
Everyone kind of
intuitively understands

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.280
that in order for
me to be effective,

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.410
I need a very mobile
workforce, and I

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.290
need everyone in my realization
to be mobile capable.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.420
However, what ends
up happening is

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.405
you slip you select a
subset of your organization

00:04:03.405 --> 00:04:04.530
to have these capabilities.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.260
You say, oh, the folks at
headquarters, the knowledge

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.233
workers, the C
level folks, these

00:04:09.233 --> 00:04:11.400
are the people who really,
really need to have this.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.920
And what ends up happening is
you have this isolated group

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.180
that has mobile capabilities.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.057
But when they try to
take action against it,

00:04:17.057 --> 00:04:18.390
you're limited in your tool set.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.490
You don't have the ability
to actually communicate

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.740
this to the people who are
doing the work, who are out

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:23.340
in the field.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.860
And you end up in a situation
where you're challenged.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.640
And so it's not it's not like
people don't want to do this,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:30.460
they're limited.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.740
And you think about why.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.890
We think quite a bit about why,
why hasn't this proliferated?

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:36.660
And the answers
aren't surprising.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.360
It's security, it's
manageability, it's cost.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.640
These are all the reasons
why, as much as I'd

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.310
love to have the person
who's checking the meter out

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.650
in the field have
this technology, wow,

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.240
I don't really have
a good scalable

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.830
way by which to manage this.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:53.172
So we spend a ton of energy
here at Google trying to help

00:04:53.172 --> 00:04:55.505
think through that so that
you don't have this challenge

00:04:55.505 --> 00:04:58.163
and you actually have the
tools necessary to leverage

00:04:58.163 --> 00:04:59.580
the capabilities
that you've heard

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.320
about for the last few days.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.950
And this is a
conversation that you

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:08.340
hear about quite a bit with
almost any enterprise software.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:12.300
You think about the constituents
within your organization

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.490
that you need to get
over the line in order

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.410
to actually be successful.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.210
There's IT, there's
your line of business,

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.710
and there's your employee.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:23.040
I think it varies depending
on the type of technology

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.060
you're talking about,
the level of control,

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.390
the level of desire
of each organization

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.213
to push forward technology.

00:05:29.213 --> 00:05:31.380
But these are all incredibly
important constituents.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:32.940
And historically,
a lot of folks have

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.600
thought about this
as the tug of war,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.540
and I have to make this trade.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.110
I'll give you a very
specific example, security.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.755
So IT obviously wants
an incredibly locked

00:05:42.755 --> 00:05:45.670
down environment, because they
don't want any data leakage,

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:48.850
they don't want any compromise
within that ecosystem.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.840
However, the end user
doesn't want that lockdown.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.550
I personally have
had this experience,

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.967
I'm sure some of
you have as well.

00:05:54.967 --> 00:05:57.178
You get a device, it is
so incredibly locked down

00:05:57.178 --> 00:05:58.470
that you can't actually use it.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.012
You shove it in your
drawer, and then

00:06:00.012 --> 00:06:01.208
you actually have shadow IT.

00:06:01.208 --> 00:06:03.750
You have a problem where people
are bringing their own device

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:04.830
and their own services.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.872
And they circumvent that
control that you thought

00:06:06.872 --> 00:06:08.070
was so fantastic.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.470
So beyond that,
when you think even

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.150
beyond that to the
line of business,

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:15.770
you think about the person who's
leading marketing, the person

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.610
who's leading distribution
and supply chain,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.550
someone who actually
then has to manage that,

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.260
if that organization
is so locked down

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.570
and their employees aren't
using that mobile device,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.690
their application
actually is unsuccessful.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:27.190
Right?

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:28.630
At the end, a very
concrete example--

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:30.005
if you have
Salesforce and nobody

00:06:30.005 --> 00:06:31.680
is updating it
because the only way

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.960
you can access it is
this purpose-built device

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.840
that no one wants to use, that
tool isn't actually useful

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.250
for you.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.440
So what we think
about then is, how

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.650
do I help security
be top of mind

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.530
but yet allow the end
user and line of business

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.410
the flexibility to
leverage that platform?

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.350
So when we think about that, we
came up with the work profile.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.020
So rather than have a very
binary choices to lock down

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.330
your device or not, we
partition the device

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.910
into a workset of
applications and experiences,

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:03.540
and then you have a
personal set of experiences.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.390
And what that allows you to
do then is rather than have

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.280
two separate devices
of which maybe I

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.688
have work applications
on my personal device

00:07:10.688 --> 00:07:12.480
and personal applications
in my work device

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.330
or I don't use the work device
at all, I'm now in a situation

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.040
where I have this
one device where

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.920
I have comfort in knowing
that IT is not monitoring

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.190
my personal use, and yet, I
have a very secure container

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.460
within the phone that IT is
very comfortable then deploying

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.200
very specific work
applications that

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.260
don't bleed data back and
forth between the two profiles.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.320
So that is an example
that when we fundamentally

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.420
think bottoms up
at Google of how

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.060
to solve the problem of
proliferating mobility

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:42.240
throughout your enterprise, we
actually designed the solution

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.420
to meet that need versus
trying to work our way back

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.130
from a feature set.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:50.070
So this applies
across all the needs

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.760
that when we think about
an IT organization trying

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.890
to think through
security and management,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.080
and we think about employees
thinking about privacy

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.600
and well-being, and we think
about lines of business that

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:02.543
actually want to
ensure that they have

00:08:02.543 --> 00:08:03.960
a platform under
which they can be

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.790
successful with
their transformation,

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.380
we focus on all of these things.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.350
And in order for
us to do that, we

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.800
think both about the ecosystem,
as well as the things

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.450
that Google controls.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.620
So the ecosystem
for Google-- we're

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.150
very much about
an open ecosystem,

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.710
consistent from
Android Enterprises

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.168
to all the technologies
that you've

00:08:21.168 --> 00:08:23.550
heard about over the past week.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:26.520
However, we understand that
there are minimum needs

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.950
that we need consistency
around in order

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:32.909
to ensure that the base level
concerns that an enterprise has

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:33.789
are met.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:35.455
And so we think
through Google Play,

00:08:35.455 --> 00:08:37.830
we think through hardware, we
think through the operating

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:40.710
system, and the ecosystem
can customize around that.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:42.179
But there are very
core components

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:45.540
that don't compromise security
when you try to deploy this

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:47.880
within the enterprise.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.520
What this allows us to do
is you now have security,

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:52.200
you now have a
flexible platform,

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.870
but you also have a
variety of form factors to,

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.050
again, think through all of
the needs of your organization.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:08:59.760
If you're trying
to deploy mobility

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.740
across your organization,
if you think

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.900
you have a mining platform
out in the Bering Sea,

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:08.300
you can't use a very high cost
device that's very fragile.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.520
It just doesn't work.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.850
However, you want that to
be part of your platform

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.058
when you have knowledge
workers in your organization.

00:09:14.058 --> 00:09:16.230
You don't want to have
islands of devices

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.920
that you're managing separately.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.460
So when we think about the
platform and the ability

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.220
to go end-to-end from
smartphones and knowledge

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.650
workers out to signage,
a point-of-sale system,

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.740
out to virtual reality,
that allows an organization

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.590
to actually deploy once, think
through the entire platform,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.400
and actually build on top of it.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:37.080
And when you have that,
we're able to unlock

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.630
a lot of the tools that
you've been hearing about,

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.410
again, in the past week.

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:44.040
You now are able to leverage ML.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.700
You're now able to leverage
development tools that allow

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.237
you to cross operating systems.

00:09:49.237 --> 00:09:51.570
These are all things that we
want to ensure your ability

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:54.570
to do because we know you have
a very diverse set of needs,

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.480
and it's not just about one
tool, one device, one platform.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.810
We also think through
this to ensure

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:06.170
that we want you to have a
very seamless experience when

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:06.890
you deploy this.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:11.540
Even though we have minimum
consistency from a security

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:13.310
perspective, we
also want to ensure

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:15.980
that when you're going out
and going into the ecosystem

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.627
to procure your devices,
to procure services,

00:10:18.627 --> 00:10:20.960
we want to make sure that you
feel comfortable that they

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:22.543
actually understand
Android Enterprise

00:10:22.543 --> 00:10:24.030
and can help you be successful.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.840
So we developed a program called
Android Enterprise Recommended

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.910
that begins to span
that value chain.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.500
You now know when you
go purchase a device,

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:32.398
it's recommended.

00:10:32.398 --> 00:10:33.440
It's certified by Google.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.302
You now know that when you
go into an EMM or a UEM,

00:10:37.302 --> 00:10:38.760
we've also certified
that, as well.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.420
So all the controls and
security that we talk about

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:43.425
are now leveragable by
you as an organization

00:10:43.425 --> 00:10:45.050
through these tools
that we've reviewed

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.670
and partner with quite
closely within the ecosystem.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.580
So now when you
purchase and you try

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:51.950
to think through an
end-to-end solution,

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:54.590
you now have certification
across the entire ecosystem

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.650
so that you don't have to
worry about validating that

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:00.240
within your own organization.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:05.750
So with that, I want
to introduce Nisha.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.720
She's thought through a
lot of these situations

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:11.822
in her experience
with Accenture.

00:11:11.822 --> 00:11:13.280
What we've talked
about quite a bit

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:16.200
now are things that have been
happening in the recent past,

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.740
as well as what people
are going through today.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:20.330
Obviously, in her
role at Accenture,

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.240
she now is able to think
through things even beyond that,

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.390
think what happens
in a world when

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:28.550
you go beyond just
having mobility

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:30.292
and you go beyond
just cloud computing.

00:11:30.292 --> 00:11:32.750
How does this actually transform
the business and the world

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:35.240
that we live in?

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:36.740
We want to be sure
that you actually

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.240
have an understanding
of that so you

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.180
can contextualize the broad
as well as the near-term.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:42.070
Thank you very much.

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:43.278
NISHA SHARMA: Thanks, Eugene.

00:11:43.278 --> 00:11:43.945
EUGENE YEH: Yep.

00:11:43.945 --> 00:11:45.070
NISHA SHARMA: Hi, everyone.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:45.830
I'm Nisha Sharma.

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:48.650
I'm a managing director
at Accenture Digital.

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:51.950
I've actually been at Accenture
for about 20 years now,

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:55.700
and about 15 of those years have
been in the enterprise mobility

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:59.360
space, so the very early days
of mobile, which I'm sure many

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:00.980
of you can remember.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:05.090
So it's been quite fascinating
to see how mobility has changed

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:06.840
over the course of this time.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.400
And what I wanted to do was
spend some time talking to you

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:13.200
about what's next.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.980
What are the trends
that we are seeing

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:20.150
within technology, within our
solutions, and the kind of work

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:23.330
that we're doing
with our clients now?

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:28.130
So every year, Accenture
releases our Technology Vision.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:33.380
We just released our most recent
2019 Tech Vision in February.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.990
And the Tech Vision
talks about, again,

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:38.660
what are some of the trends that
we are seeing in the business?

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:41.990
Not just technology
trends, but trends

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:45.020
that are impacting
the kind of work

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:47.190
that our clients are doing.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.430
And so I thought I'd
spend a little bit

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:53.180
of time sharing that with you
so you can understand where

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:55.700
mobility fits,
where Android fits,

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:59.630
and the kinds of things that
we are doing in this space

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:03.830
because it's changing quite
quickly, and you'll see that.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:06.740
So just to kind
of set the stage,

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:09.980
the digital era
technology that we've been

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:11.540
talking about all this time--

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:14.280
it's expected now
from every business.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:18.350
In fact, in 2018, companies
spent $1.1 trillion

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:21.320
on digital
transformation projects,

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:23.810
and 94% of organizations
say that they're

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:26.750
doing digital
transformation work already.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:30.730
So it's just expected.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:31.740
So what's next?

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.270
If everybody's doing this,
what are the next types

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:35.690
of things that we're working on?

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:38.800
And we call this now
the post-digital era.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:41.840
And again, the
digital technologies--

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.870
it doesn't mean that
digital is over.

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:45.050
It's certainly not the case.

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:48.400
It just means that digital
is just expected to be there.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:49.880
It's the price of admission.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:54.160
We don't say anymore--
we don't ask, well,

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:56.470
if you have a mobile
phone, then do this.

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:59.560
It's just, on your
mobile phone, do this.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:03.470
So it's just assumed
that you have this.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.980
So we're starting to see a
new set of characteristics

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:10.240
that are defining what this
post-digital era looks like.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:13.150
And some of those things are
around individualization,

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:14.620
as an example.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:18.790
It's about knowing
what our users want.

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:21.250
It's not just
personalization, but it's

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:24.380
almost hyper-personalization.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:24.880
OK.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:29.860
I know you like these things, or
you've done things around this,

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:32.650
but it's really
understanding what you want

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:34.100
and what you need.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.670
It's also around
instant on-demand.

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:40.540
So yeah, OK, we know what you
want, but you want it now.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:41.920
You need it now.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:45.740
So how do we get these
services to you in that way?

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:47.710
We also talk about
momentary markets,

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:50.930
how it's almost this
pop-up-like environment.

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:53.230
And I'll give you this
example of some work

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.600
that we've been doing with
Carnival Cruise Lines.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.220
They're transforming the
entire ship experience.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:04.180
And I don't know if anybody's
been on a recent Carnival

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.750
Cruise experience, but
what they're doing now

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:10.390
is everybody gets a
medallion, they call it.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:11.960
It's a wearable device.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.152
So this medallion
knows your preferences,

00:15:14.152 --> 00:15:15.610
it knows where you
are on the ship,

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.720
it knows what you're
doing, and all of that.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.780
It allows you to buy things.

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:22.030
It's like that.

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:25.090
And in addition to that,
they've got sensors

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:26.430
all around the ship.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:32.770
The cruise employees all have
mobile devices with them.

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:36.737
And they've got all this
analytics and everything just

00:15:36.737 --> 00:15:37.570
all around the ship.

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:41.580
So the idea is that
let's say there's

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:44.860
an attraction, like
a show or something,

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.960
and they realize that based
on everything that they know,

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:49.960
that there's an upcoming
attraction that perhaps

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:52.717
your child might be
interested in going,

00:15:52.717 --> 00:15:54.300
that you know that
they want to go to.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:57.410
So now they can just send
you a message saying,

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:59.440
hey, I've reserved
a spot for you.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.710
Would you like to attend this?

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:06.263
Or would you like to
purchase this ticket to go?

00:16:06.263 --> 00:16:07.680
And it gives you
that opportunity.

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.220
So it's very momentary.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:11.102
It's just, hey, you
weren't planning this,

00:16:11.102 --> 00:16:12.310
but this opportunity came up.

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:15.440
I'd like to give you the
opportunity to buy this,

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:16.930
and then it's done.

00:16:16.930 --> 00:16:20.790
So that's what we mean
by momentary markets.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:24.010
And the other characteristic
is around trust

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:24.850
and responsibility.

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:29.580
We're just expecting trust
from all of our providers.

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:35.210
So much data is being collected
and being available about us,

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.680
and so it's just expected
that in this new world,

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:45.070
that companies are treating
our data responsibly and making

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:49.100
sure that we understand,
also, how they're using it.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:50.770
So what I want to
do is take a look

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:53.830
at some of the trends that
are in the Tech Vision.

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:57.100
Now, there are five trends
that we talk about overall,

00:16:57.100 --> 00:16:59.260
but I'd like to really
focus on three of them

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:03.400
because I think they are
most impacting the space

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:05.230
around enterprise mobility.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:08.089
And it'll show you what's
happening in the enterprise

00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:08.589
world.

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:10.359
Some of the other
trends are more around

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.069
the consumer experience
and what we're

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:13.690
seeing in the consumer world.

00:17:13.690 --> 00:17:17.819
But I thought I'd start
with the enterprise side.

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:22.200
So the first trend is called
DARQ power-- "dark" with a Q.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:23.819
And we'll go through
each of these,

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:27.140
but it's Distributed Ledgers,
Artificial Intelligence,

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.330
Extended Reality, and
Quantum Computing.

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:32.270
These are basically the
new wave of technologies

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:36.170
that we're seeing
our clients use now.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:39.410
So the D is for
distributed ledgers.

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:41.330
We're using technologies
like blockchain,

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:44.060
and cryptocurrencies, and
these types of things.

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:45.770
We're using them in
our supply chains

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:51.240
to have end-to-end trusted
transactions and such.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:54.680
We have carmakers who are
using blockchain technologies

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:57.080
to protect cars from hackers.

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:59.240
We have delivery
companies, like DHL

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.820
for example, who
are using blockchain

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:06.020
to ensure that perhaps some
medication that you ordered

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.420
is the correct medication,
and it's not counterfeit,

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:09.980
and following it along the way.

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:14.630
So we're seeing the uses of
those types of technologies.

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:17.010
The A is for artificial
intelligence.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.550
I'm sure you've seen so
many examples of some really

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:22.790
cool artificial intelligence
and machine learning

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:27.380
types of implementations,
even here at Next.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:32.260
In fact, at our booth,
we have the robot--

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:35.990
if you've seen the robotic arm
that is taking Petri dishes

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.230
and putting them under
a camera for analysis

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:42.440
and is using image
recognition to check

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:46.910
the quality of those
samples, and then it's

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:49.610
got the new IoT Edge
TPU incorporated

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:52.340
in that to accelerate
that kind of processing.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:55.310
But so there's a lot that's
happening in the AI space

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:57.960
around image recognition,
natural language processing,

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:00.060
and so on and so on.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:02.570
In fact, I think
67% of businesses

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:04.880
say that they're
already exploring

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:07.610
artificial intelligence
in some ways today.

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:09.620
The R is for extended reality--

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:11.660
XR.

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:14.060
That's augmented
reality, virtual reality,

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:17.320
mixed realities, assisted
reality, so on and so on.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:19.400
And this is all about
new experiences.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.770
It's about new ways of training.

00:19:21.770 --> 00:19:25.790
We're now providing our
workers with headsets

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:30.320
that allow them to
virtualize environments

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:35.660
so that they can train and learn
in those places, as an example.

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:37.760
New ways of shopping
I'm sure you've seen.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:39.680
I mean, you take
your phone and you

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:42.080
can augment some product
that's out there,

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.120
or be able to interact
with some product,

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:46.040
perhaps, in a virtual way.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:49.070
New ways of exploring
places for education

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:52.520
and so on, just to be
able to virtually see

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:57.800
in a national park, or a
museum, or someplace like that.

00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:00.920
Then the Q is for
quantum computing.

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:03.210
It seems like quantum
computing is far out,

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:05.810
but we actually are working
with some of our clients

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:08.750
now to explore how
quantum computing can

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:12.410
be used to solve some difficult
problems that they perhaps

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:14.630
weren't able to solve before.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.322
We worked with a
company called 1QBit,

00:20:17.322 --> 00:20:18.530
if you're familiar with them.

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:21.050
But we mapped out
over 150 different use

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.810
cases for quantum computing--

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:27.170
things like drug discovery,
or route optimization,

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:31.410
or fraud detection, and
those types of scenarios.

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:34.670
So individually,
these are definitely

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.570
opportunities for
differentiation

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:38.430
in this new world.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:40.200
And then when you
put them together,

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:44.510
you really, really start to
see some new, even unimagined,

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:49.760
possibilities around what
we can do in the future.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:53.360
So we talked about the future,
and these are not way out

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:54.530
future trends.

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:56.970
These are actually
already happening now.

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:59.780
89% of businesses say
that they're already

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.390
experimenting with one
or more of these types

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:04.190
of DARQ technologies.

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:07.520
And businesses are
expecting big impacts

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:08.790
from these technologies.

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:11.960
Just as an example,
41% of executives

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:14.540
rank AI as the
technology that they

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:17.990
expect to have the most
impact in their organization

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:19.560
over the next three years.

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:23.210
So people are doing this,
and it's happening now.

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:24.710
But again, like I
said, that doesn't

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:28.760
mean that everything we've done
with the digital technologies

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:29.880
is done.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:34.370
In fact, we're expecting
that those are the foundation

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:36.200
for these new technologies.

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:40.130
So we talk about here this
move from SMAC to DARQ.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:42.770
Have you all heard of SMAC--

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:46.370
Social, Mobile, Analytics,
and Cloud technologies.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:49.460
And up until now, that's the
kind of work we had been doing.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:52.040
We had been using social
media technologies.

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:54.980
We've been rolling
out mobile devices.

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:58.280
Maybe not those 41% of
companies that you mentioned,

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:02.720
Eugene, but companies
really are using

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:05.040
these, both in the
consumer space,

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:08.480
but also in the
enterprise space.

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:13.340
And then cloud and analytics,
and just again as an example,

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:17.520
as we move to these dark
technologies, mobile is just--

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:18.600
it's the front-end.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:20.950
It's the channel that
people will be using.

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:24.150
It's just expected to be there.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:26.920
And we'll talk
about that, as well.

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:34.700
Again, just another example,
that 92% percent of companies

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:38.690
there say that their SMAC
technologies are still

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:41.570
considered to be a
competitive differentiation.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:45.290
But we are moving past
that and establishing

00:22:45.290 --> 00:22:47.480
that as a foundation,
and it's only

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:50.840
going to allow us to take
our businesses in the future.

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:54.480
And the whole idea, really, is
that if you're not doing these,

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:58.400
if you haven't already
done the SMAC technologies,

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:01.550
it's going to be harder for
you to keep up, especially

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:04.570
as we're moving very, very
quickly into the future.

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:06.530
As we all know, the
pace of innovation,

00:23:06.530 --> 00:23:09.020
the pace of technology
changes is just

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:11.030
continuing to
accelerate all the time.

00:23:14.570 --> 00:23:18.290
So just using the
mobility example here,

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:21.040
so we talked about
XR, extended reality.

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:27.130
And this example is just
showing that virtual reality

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:30.580
and augmented reality
solutions are all

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:32.560
built on mobile devices.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:33.940
You need a mobile device.

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:36.770
You need the network
communication.

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:40.900
You need to have applications
that are built to support this,

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:43.370
and they're all running
on mobile devices, just as

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:43.870
an example.

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:50.540
So the next trend I want to talk
about is what we call Human+.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:53.990
So again, talking about what
is impacting our enterprises,

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:56.730
well, this is about
our workforce.

00:23:56.730 --> 00:24:01.400
And what we're seeing
is that 90% of jobs

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:03.050
that we currently
have today, we know

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:06.890
that they're going to change
as a result of AI, robotics,

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:08.990
and other such technologies.

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:11.030
So jobs are changing fast.

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:12.350
And what we're seeing is that--

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:16.880
what we know is that
individuals, as workers,

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:20.790
we of course come with our
skills and with our knowledge,

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:25.580
but we are also becoming human+
in that we are enabled even

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:29.600
further by technology that our
companies are providing to us

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:30.770
to do our work.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:36.590
And I'll show you a couple
of examples of that.

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:39.350
But the point is that as
this workforce evolves,

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:41.420
our companies need
to keep up with that

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:44.540
and be able to provide the
employees with the tools

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:49.050
that they want and need
in order to do their jobs.

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:53.650
So a couple of examples
here just to highlight.

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:57.230
We have an oil and gas company
whose workers right now have

00:24:57.230 --> 00:24:59.030
mobile devices and
mobile technologies

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:01.070
to help them
troubleshoot issues that

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:04.130
are a mile underground
with these new game-like

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.490
visualization tools that have
been developed for them to do

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:08.930
that troubleshooting.

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:14.060
We have factories where
humans and robots are working

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:17.810
side-by-side in the
factories to do the work,

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:21.170
and we have AI tools
that are helping

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:23.600
to identify which
types of jobs should be

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:26.450
done by which types of workers.

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:29.060
We have workers who are being
trained as drone delivery

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:29.750
pilots.

00:25:29.750 --> 00:25:32.190
These are jobs that
never existed before.

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:34.640
But again, they
would be able to use

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:37.130
the technology and the
applications and such

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:39.380
that their workers have
that their employers have

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:42.500
given them to be able to
work with these drones,

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:44.090
and analyze what's
coming from them,

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:50.900
and be able to support
the work in those ways.

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:55.040
But when it comes
to what companies

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:59.000
need to be thinking about in
order to support their workers,

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:00.800
we're really looking
at three areas

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:03.170
and these are around hiring,
training, and knowledge

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:04.530
management.

00:26:04.530 --> 00:26:08.000
So if you think
about it from hiring,

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:13.040
when it comes to these changing
jobs, this changing workforce,

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:15.920
it's getting harder and
harder to find the people who

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:17.210
have these skills already.

00:26:20.505 --> 00:26:24.962
And so companies are shifting
the normal approaches

00:26:24.962 --> 00:26:26.420
that they would
use to hire people.

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:28.003
So they're moving
away from these more

00:26:28.003 --> 00:26:31.190
kind of reactive skills-based
hiring approaches,

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:34.550
not just like putting out a
job description that says,

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:37.610
I need someone who has three
years of accounting experience.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:40.410
They're not looking at
it that way anymore.

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:42.680
Now, as an example, Unilever--

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:44.180
one of the things
that they're doing

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:46.160
is they're actually
screening candidates

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:47.850
with a series of games.

00:26:47.850 --> 00:26:49.400
So imagine they've
got all these apps

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:51.290
and such that have
these games on them,

00:26:51.290 --> 00:26:53.060
and they're assessing
their memory.

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:55.393
They're assessing their
acceptance of risk.

00:26:55.393 --> 00:26:56.810
They're looking
at whether they're

00:26:56.810 --> 00:27:00.350
more likely to read
contextual or emotional cues.

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.600
So they're trying to
understand how you work,

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:04.180
rather than what you know.

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:07.700
And then they're using AI
tools to review those results

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:11.660
and match these
candidates with open jobs.

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:14.300
So again, another
example of we're

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:16.910
providing applications
on mobile devices

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:21.770
to allow the employers to do
these sorts of assessments.

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:24.800
On the training side,
again, companies

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:29.420
can't hire fast enough in
terms of the kinds of jobs

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:30.950
that we need skills for.

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:34.460
And so there's a need to invest
in reskilling and providing

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:41.410
on-demand training opportunities
for our existing workforce.

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:44.600
This survey says that
43% of businesses

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:47.270
say that 60% of
their workforce will

00:27:47.270 --> 00:27:49.920
move into new roles over
the next three years.

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:53.540
So how do we provide our
workers with the training

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:54.650
that they need?

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:56.630
And this is again
being done with mobile

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:57.870
because they're on the go.

00:27:57.870 --> 00:27:59.930
They're just moving,
and we're really

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:02.900
finding new ways of
providing training options

00:28:02.900 --> 00:28:05.600
to our employees.

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:08.610
And the third area's around
knowledge management.

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:10.430
So we have more
information available to us

00:28:10.430 --> 00:28:13.610
than we ever have before,
but it's harder and harder

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:16.220
to find and access
this information

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:18.050
because people are
moving around quickly

00:28:18.050 --> 00:28:20.630
and it's just hard to capture.

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:24.380
So we're using new technologies,
like natural language

00:28:24.380 --> 00:28:26.870
processing, as a way to
capture this information

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:29.970
and be able to access it.

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:34.460
We're looking at things
like indexing unstructured

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:37.250
documents, such as an incident
report or something like that,

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:42.440
so we can find the
information we need there.

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:44.840
We're looking at
knowledge graphs

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:49.970
to be able to search a wide
variety of data sources.

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:55.580
And so again, we're looking
at really changing our tools

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:57.950
that we've been
using for so long

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:01.260
to be able to use
them in new ways.

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:03.920
So I think another
point, as well,

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:06.630
when it comes to
hiring and training,

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:09.470
one of the things that
businesses are also looking at

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:11.960
is the new generation
of workers that

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:14.090
are coming into the workforce.

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:16.010
They're being forced
to look at, what

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:18.590
are the tools and technologies
that they're already

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:23.330
using in the world, and
recognizing in our world today,

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:26.180
the majority of people
have Android devices.

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:28.140
And so companies are looking--

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:29.900
OK, well, let's take
advantage of that.

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:32.900
Let's build on these Android
experiences and the Android

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:37.010
devices that they have to
be able to provide them

00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:38.480
with the same types
of experiences

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:42.650
that they're used to, or even
tools like G Suite, where I'm

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:46.130
sure you've seen that
in schools and colleges,

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:47.780
students are using G Suite.

00:29:47.780 --> 00:29:51.395
So how do we build these
new tools and capabilities

00:29:51.395 --> 00:29:53.270
on things that they
already know, and they're

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:56.560
comfortable with, and they like,
rather than thinking of, oh,

00:29:56.560 --> 00:30:00.080
what are those old-school,
more traditional tools,

00:30:00.080 --> 00:30:04.650
they really have to think
about these in new ways.

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:07.370
So the third trend that
I wanted to talk about

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:09.410
is secure us to secure me.

00:30:09.410 --> 00:30:11.720
This is all about security.

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:15.380
And the point here is that
security is no longer just

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:19.970
about protecting one's self,
it's about protecting everyone.

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:23.750
And we can't, as a business--

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:25.900
it still typically
sees cybersecurity

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:28.670
as an individual effort,
meaning that if I

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:32.370
secure my own systems,
if I secure my own data,

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:33.380
then I'll be safe.

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:36.180
But that's really not the
case because as businesses,

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:39.230
we are part of a
broader ecosystem, now,

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:41.720
of technology partners
that we're working with.

00:30:41.720 --> 00:30:45.690
And every technology, every
solution, every application,

00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:47.350
no matter where
it's coming from,

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:51.530
can be exposed to
vulnerabilities in some way.

00:30:51.530 --> 00:30:54.080
And so you can't just
assume that, OK, well,

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:55.470
you guys have it covered.

00:30:55.470 --> 00:31:00.090
You have to work together in
this new world to do that.

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:03.020
In fact, the survey point
there says that only 29%

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:07.160
of executives actually know that
their ecosystem partners are

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:11.240
working just as diligently
as they would expect

00:31:11.240 --> 00:31:13.450
to in regards to security.

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:18.410
Now, we're seeing new types of
risks or coming up in new ways

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:19.760
around the whole misuse of data.

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:21.560
Normally, we think
that if someone

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:23.635
gets unauthorized
access to my data, well,

00:31:23.635 --> 00:31:25.760
then they're going to use
that to exploit something

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:26.780
in my system.

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:29.430
But in reality, what's happening
is you can find this data,

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:32.300
you can find data in
some other system that's

00:31:32.300 --> 00:31:36.560
totally unrelated to you, but
use it somehow to infiltrate

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:38.510
or get into your own systems.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:41.480
So the attackers are
really now seeing

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:47.610
this ecosystem as an
ever-widening attack surface.

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:50.030
Another example is
around aggregated data.

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:52.700
We think of aggregated data
as being somewhat anonymized,

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:56.682
or can't necessarily tell
who, identify individuals,

00:31:56.682 --> 00:31:57.640
or something like that.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:31:59.140
But we've seen cases
where there was

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:02.930
an example of some aggregated
data that was found on Strava

00:32:02.930 --> 00:32:06.410
and then used out of context
to identify some secret US

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:07.490
military sites.

00:32:07.490 --> 00:32:12.540
So in today's connected
and ecosystem world,

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:15.050
the impact of cyber
attacks is just

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:17.270
being exponentially amplified.

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:21.770
And you might remember
though the WannaCry crypto

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:23.240
worm that happened.

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:26.810
That was where someone
exposed an operating system

00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:32.360
vulnerability and attacked
300,000 computers over 150

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:34.835
countries in just a few days.

00:32:34.835 --> 00:32:36.710
And that brought down
all sorts of businesses

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:39.830
and impacted our
work tremendously.

00:32:39.830 --> 00:32:47.360
There was the Mirai malware that
hijacked 100,000 IoT devices,

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:50.600
and then they use that to
launch an attack on a domain

00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:52.340
registration services provider.

00:32:52.340 --> 00:32:55.220
So there's these
downstream impacts

00:32:55.220 --> 00:32:58.940
and all sorts of impacts that
we have as a result of us

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:00.830
being in this world.

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:04.850
And what I think
businesses are realizing

00:33:04.850 --> 00:33:08.300
is that as they collaborate
with their ecosystem partners

00:33:08.300 --> 00:33:11.640
on new services, and new
products, and new experiences,

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:14.540
they also really need to be
collaborating on security, as

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:15.330
well.

00:33:15.330 --> 00:33:19.160
And there's no doubt that
Google, with Android and Google

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:23.180
Cloud, are a part of this
right, because the foundation

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:27.020
of a lot of what we do
is on our mobile device.

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:30.620
And as employees,
as consumers, we

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:34.890
don't necessarily know exactly
where something went wrong.

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:37.370
It's like, oh, something
happened on my phone.

00:33:37.370 --> 00:33:39.680
And people will just
think of, OK, it's

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:41.260
an Android problem or whatever.

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:44.420
But that's why Google and
the Android Enterprise team

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:47.600
have been doing so
much to try to protect

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:49.820
the experience on
the phones, not

00:33:49.820 --> 00:33:54.860
just, I have to protect my piece
here, but recognize their role

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:57.290
in this ecosystem
and in the solutions

00:33:57.290 --> 00:33:59.210
that we're providing
to everyone,

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:03.380
and doing what they
can to protect us.

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:08.940
Again, to protect everyone,
not just as individuals.

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:11.905
So yeah, so I think these
are just some of the trends

00:34:11.905 --> 00:34:14.030
that I wanted to highlight
for you just to give you

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:16.909
a glimpse of what's
happening out there

00:34:16.909 --> 00:34:20.960
as you think about the future
and how we can continue

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:24.199
to use mobile, and how it is
such a foundation for what

00:34:24.199 --> 00:34:25.739
we are doing.

00:34:25.739 --> 00:34:28.590
These are the other trends here.

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:30.760
If you want to learn more,
you can visit our site,

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:36.980
accenture.com/technologyvision,
but yeah, hopefully that helps.

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:39.530
So Eugene?

00:34:39.530 --> 00:34:40.039
Thank you.

00:34:40.039 --> 00:34:43.389
[MUSIC PLAYING]

